WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.280
So this one is how to model a

00:00:03.280 --> 00:00:05.030
lightning strike.

00:00:07.359 --> 00:00:08.960
We're going to try and make an animation

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:10.480
of a lightning strike that looks

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:11.280
realistic.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.920
And I'm not claiming that it is a

00:00:13.920 --> 00:00:15.980
realistic model of lightning-

00:00:19.359 --> 00:00:21.279
This is- this is kind of secretly a

00:00:21.279 --> 00:00:23.920
demonstration of an algorithm in

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.640
computer science called 
Breadth-First Search

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.800
which we'll be showing as like a maze

00:00:28.800 --> 00:00:30.560
solving algorithm.

00:00:30.560 --> 00:00:33.840
So what we have is a it's a frontier of

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:37.120
exploration, bits of lightning, and one of

00:00:37.120 --> 00:00:38.000
them

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:40.079
hits the ground first and that- the path

00:00:40.079 --> 00:00:42.960
that that one followed ends up being the

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:45.280
shape of the lightning strike.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:48.239
So like a frontier of exploration

00:00:48.239 --> 00:00:50.160
and as soon as you find it

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:51.600
that one gets reinforced and that

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.399
becomes a lightning strike. And

00:00:54.399 --> 00:00:57.280
this is taking place inside a randomly

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.520
generated labyrinth.

00:00:59.520 --> 00:01:02.079
So maybe the walls inside this maze

00:01:02.079 --> 00:01:04.000
would correspond to fluctuations in the

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.920
air, like variations in the

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:08.400
resistance or how charges can pass

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:09.439
through

00:01:09.439 --> 00:01:12.240
the air; and maybe if air was perfectly

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:14.000
homogeneous it would just be- lightning

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.200
would always be a straight line, I don't

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:15.920
know.

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:17.640
(Brady: It's really good looking
this one isn't it?)

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:18.799
(Were you pleased with how it)

00:01:18.799 --> 00:01:20.240
(turned out once you'd made it? Did you)

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:22.000
(think, wow that looks awesome!)
- Yeah I

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.080
think it's quite, like, satisfying I

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:26.159
think. There's a- there's a bit of- there's

00:01:26.159 --> 00:01:27.840
a kind of game at the beginning where

00:01:27.840 --> 00:01:28.960
you're like well which one's going to

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:31.280
win? And you know in some of these you've

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:32.960
got two kind of

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:35.600
two or three competing parts, and is this

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:36.720
one going to win? Is this is going to win?

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:38.720
Then that one wins in the end and then

00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:41.280
you you have the satisfying bolt of

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:44.700
lightning at the end. 
- (Your cat seems really into it.)

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:46.560
Yeah,

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:47.680
helps me out with all of these

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:50.880
animations. This is done in Mathematica.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.840
The first stage is generating the random

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:54.799
maze,

00:01:54.799 --> 00:01:57.040
then there's solving the maze, and then

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:59.520
there's generating the animation from

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.280
the solution. So here we are in

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:03.600
Mathematica. This is code to randomly

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:05.600
generate a maze, which we're visualising

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.560
here. So maybe you can see if I run this

00:02:08.560 --> 00:02:09.920
every time,

00:02:09.920 --> 00:02:11.680
I'm going to get a random

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:13.520
configuration

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:16.000
of of walls in the maze.
- (How do you know)

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:18.480
(the maze is always going to be solvable?)

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:20.160
What I would do is just generate it

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:22.480
again again until it is solvable. Since

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.680
it's random

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:24.959
basically it turns out it'd be quite

00:02:24.959 --> 00:02:26.239
unlucky

00:02:26.239 --> 00:02:27.760
if it's not solvable, but I could be

00:02:27.760 --> 00:02:28.959
unlucky and then

00:02:28.959 --> 00:02:30.000
the-

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:32.080
the output of the breadth-first search

00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:33.920
will be there is no solution to this

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:35.599
maze and I'll say, okay let's generate

00:02:35.599 --> 00:02:37.840
another random one. The way this works is

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.440
that we're going to be working on a

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:41.360
square grid. Here are all the points that

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.239
might be connected by lines of a square;

00:02:44.239 --> 00:02:45.519
and then each

00:02:45.519 --> 00:02:46.800
each of these

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:50.319
potential lines here will either be free

00:02:50.319 --> 00:02:53.280
or there will be a wall of the maze. You

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.120
know there's one probability for each of

00:02:55.120 --> 00:02:56.720
these

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.400
being

00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:00.720
joined- being joined, yeah, and then

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.840
there's another- there's a different

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:06.000
probability for the horizontal ones. And

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:08.319
I did that just because I wanted to make

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:10.560
it more likely to be solvable.

00:03:10.560 --> 00:03:12.800
So these have a these have a different

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.519
probability, q say, of being

00:03:15.519 --> 00:03:18.879
joined and then I just set it so that,

00:03:18.879 --> 00:03:21.360
you know, I'd set it so that q

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:24.080
was less likely than than p. 
- (There are)

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.839
(more vertical lines
than horizontal lines.)

00:03:25.839 --> 00:03:27.040
Yeah there should be, but there

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.720
might not be.

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:30.959
So then there is the-

00:03:30.959 --> 00:03:32.400
the step to solve

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:35.280
the maze. You know that's this code here.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.760
It's relatively easy to just sort of

00:03:37.760 --> 00:03:40.560
show how it works I think? So say we have

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:41.519
some

00:03:41.519 --> 00:03:42.879
maze and

00:03:42.879 --> 00:03:44.159
what we're asking is we're going to

00:03:44.159 --> 00:03:47.280
start at a point at the top, right?

00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:50.799
And our goal is to get down to

00:03:50.799 --> 00:03:52.959
the bottom. So, you know, lightning is

00:03:52.959 --> 00:03:54.239
somewhere in the cloud and we just need

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:56.000
to get anywhere on the ground. We've got

00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:57.200
various

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.640
walls that are

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:01.840
joined - this might be enough to get

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:04.640
the idea. You know theoretically we could

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.720
just solve this by trying all possible

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:09.040
paths to the bottom, but we could be more

00:04:09.040 --> 00:04:11.360
efficient than that. And the the idea is

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:12.799
that we're just going to take it one

00:04:12.799 --> 00:04:14.400
step at a time. We know that we're

00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:15.519
starting

00:04:15.519 --> 00:04:16.479
in this

00:04:16.479 --> 00:04:19.359
cell, so we can get there in zero steps.

00:04:19.359 --> 00:04:20.799
Now we can get to

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:23.199
this one in one step, and get to this

00:04:23.199 --> 00:04:25.520
cell in two steps, we can get to this cell

00:04:25.520 --> 00:04:28.479
in three steps and to 
this cell in four steps.

00:04:28.479 --> 00:04:30.800
Now we've got a branching point. And this

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:32.479
might be where you would be worried that,

00:04:32.479 --> 00:04:34.240
okay now our code is going to go off in

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:38.160
this- and go be intractable. But really

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.600
let's just take it one step at a time

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:42.720
and just record how how far we can get.

00:04:42.720 --> 00:04:44.560
This cell we can get to in five steps

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.160
and we can also get to this one in five

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:48.960
steps. Now where can we get to in six

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:51.680
steps? We can get here, we can get here,

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:54.320
or we can get here. And how far can we

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:56.160
get in seven steps? You know, there's a

00:04:56.160 --> 00:04:58.320
box up here we can get to; here we could

00:04:58.320 --> 00:05:01.440
go right or down; here we could go down.

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:02.720
You know, and eventually we're going to

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:04.560
have some kind of path that goes

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:07.039
something like this. Now that we've got

00:05:07.039 --> 00:05:10.080
to the bottom, what 
that's told us is

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:11.680
there's a path to the bottom that only

00:05:11.680 --> 00:05:14.000
takes 10 steps. So let's not bother

00:05:14.000 --> 00:05:16.320
looking at all the 11 steps ones because

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:17.919
we know how to get there in 10, all we

00:05:17.919 --> 00:05:19.480
need is the shortest path.

00:05:21.759 --> 00:05:24.136
It's called breadth-first search.

00:05:24.136 --> 00:05:26.800
(Breadth-first search?)
- Yeah breadth-first; and

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.039
that's as opposed to depth-first.
- (What)

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:31.120
(happens in your code if there's a tie?)

00:05:31.120 --> 00:05:33.360
Yeah that's a good question. In that case

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:36.160
it will just be down to

00:05:36.160 --> 00:05:37.919
basically random or the way that I

00:05:37.919 --> 00:05:40.160
implemented the code. You know, maybe I

00:05:40.160 --> 00:05:42.639
looked at paths to the left before it's

00:05:42.639 --> 00:05:44.960
passed down or to the right, and so it'll

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.840
just pick a tie. So yeah it's not- so it's

00:05:47.840 --> 00:05:51.120
it's ambiguous, yeah. So here,

00:05:51.120 --> 00:05:52.800
you know, before we generated one and it

00:05:52.800 --> 00:05:55.199
looks like we were lucky and there is a

00:05:55.199 --> 00:05:56.960
path that goes down.

00:05:56.960 --> 00:05:59.360
So yeah, so this was the original maze

00:05:59.360 --> 00:06:02.000
and now this is solving the maze and

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:03.840
just visualising the path

00:06:03.840 --> 00:06:04.880
which,

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:07.199
it's maybe a bit hard to see because

00:06:07.199 --> 00:06:08.240
it doesn't

00:06:08.240 --> 00:06:11.680
correspond with- with that but

00:06:11.680 --> 00:06:14.160
you know maybe I could quickly

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:16.160
draw them on top of each other?

00:06:16.160 --> 00:06:18.319
Let's make the path red...

00:06:18.319 --> 00:06:21.039
We solved this original maze and this is-

00:06:21.039 --> 00:06:22.560
this ended up being

00:06:22.560 --> 00:06:24.960
the shortest path or one of the shortest

00:06:24.960 --> 00:06:27.199
paths. 
